name: scalar-localize
prereqs: [none — a whole-program scope census over the A1]
// proof: none yet. The Tier-1 obligation is the three-clause invariant
//   below (verdict => single scope, live at the binding site, no unmodelled
//   reach); no Isabelle session exists, so this manifest claims nothing.
invariant: a name is marked SCALAR-LOCAL only when ALL of:
    (a) every mention sits in ONE scope (`Main` or one named function/sub;
        a nested `def` body is its OWN scope, not the enclosing one),
    (b) it is written at least once with a LITERAL or non-self-referential
        RHS whose first mention is a TOP-LEVEL write of that scope — that is
        where the renderer's `let` lands (`y=$((y+2))` reads `y` first, so
        it cannot open a binding),
    (c) no construct can reach a name the walk cannot see: `eval`/`source`/
        `.` in either spelling (`Exec` or `Call{exec|builtin}`), a dynamic
        `getVar`/`setVar`/`param` name argument, `Ext`, `RawText`, `Select`,
        `Asm`, `Shaderize`.
  Loop bindings (`for i in …`, a recovered counted loop's counter) are
  excluded unconditionally: the renderer emits its own binding for them, and
  a pass-2 `let` either shadows it or leaves the assignment dangling.
  The `risky` bit of `list_profile::use_scopes` is deliberately NOT
  consulted — it describes a renderer that lowers an argument through a
  capturing file-scope helper; a backend whose reads splice inline (Rust:
  `read_str`/`getvar_str`) legitimately ignores it.
scope: offered to rs (the `__SHV_*` thread-local → `let` promotion). C has
  its own `local_owner` verdict and would be adopting a second source for the
  same fact; estree/go/perl keep variables in a runtime store, so the verdict
  has nowhere to land there today. Not forced on any backend.
updates: none (first offer)
